I. INTRODUCTION
Multisensor multitarget tracking (MTT) is a pivotal challenge across various domains, including surveillance, autonomous navigation and collaborative search [1]. The random finite sets (RFSs) theory effectively encapsulates the stochastic nature of target appearances and movements, offering a robust framework for analyzing and modeling target dynamics comprehensively [2]. The RFS includes two primary filter categories: rectangular and parametric approximation filters. The former approximates finite set characteristics using moments, whereas the latter approaches tracking by parameterizing the distribution of finite sets. The probability hypothesis density (PHD) filter and the multi-Bernoulli filter stand out due to their widespread application. The PHD filter is renowned for its dynamic adaptability to variations in target numbers, ensuring high robustness and accuracy in estimating both target quantity and spatial positions. While the multi-Bernoulli filter shows efficacy in MTT tasks, its rigidity in adapting to changing target numbers positions the PHD filter as a more flexible and suitable choice under diverse operational scenarios. The widespread availability of low-cost, power-efficient sensors, along with advancements in networked sensing technologies, has made it possible to collaboratively utilize numerous connected sensors for MTT in extensive areas [3]. However, data aggregation across multiple sensors introduces a set of complexities. Centralized models face computational bottlenecks and delays due to bandwidth limitations, necessitating a paradigm shift towards a distributed model [4]. This shift optimizes data exchange and collaboration among sensing units. Despite its advantages, distributed multitarget tracking presents several formidable challenges as follows.
1) Unknown cross-sensor covariances: In distributed tracking environments, the unknown covariances between sensors significantly hinder optimal estimation, reducing the system’s ability to accurately estimate the states of multiple targets [5]. 2) Clutter and target misidentification: The significant presence of clutter during the detection process leads to target misidentification, especially when targets first enter the sensor’s field of view (FoV) [6]. Although continuous estimation can correct this misidentification, it often results in unacceptable detection delays, particularly problematic in high-risk environments. 3) Complex fusion process: In multisensor information fusion, preprocessing through clustering is typically required [7]. As the count of neighboring nodes rises, the complexity of this clustering preprocessing frequently multiplies, resulting in substantially higher computational expenses and extended processing times.
