y integrated into the framework of the PHD filter, with implementations using particle and Gaussian mixture methods [21], [22]. Mahler [23] later expanded this domain by developing jump-Markov probability hypothesis density (JMPHD) and jump-Markov cardinalized PHD (JMCPHD) filters, which established a new theoretical foundation for these technologies. Research has since continued to refine these methods, addressing various challenges [4], [24]. However, developing a reliable and robust multisensor JMPHD filter continues to be a challenge. The article presents a method that integrates the jump-Markov system into a distributed framework and introduces a new measurement information cross-correlation (MIC) technique to estimate the positions of target appearances. This approach leverages the correlation among adjacent detections, effectively curtailing the dissemination of redundant data. The main contributions of this article are the following.
1) To address the issue of unknown covariance in sensor networks, a quantitative analysis of information
changes during the multisensor fusion process is provided in this article from the perspective of information content (IC), exploring information redundancy in overlapping FoV. This guides the development of a Gaussian component (GC) local individual fusion (IF) method that considers the characteristics of specific regions. 2) An MIC method that considers the correlation between detections from adjacent sensors in a sensor network is proposed. Before fusion, the method leverages overlapping FoV to extract key targets, treating them as additional focal points in the filter update process. This enhances the response speed to newly emerging targets while reducing the impact of clutter on system performance. 3) In the specific clustering implementation of the MIC method, a data clustering fusion approach using local sensor measurements as indices is proposed to ensure efficient information fusion. This approach specifically considers one-way data matching between local sensors and their neighbors.
The rest of this article is organized as follows. Section II provides background information. Section III presents a proof of the conservativeness of the AA method and analyzes the information volume in the fusion process. Section IV introduces the distributed multisensor information fusion framework, including the design of the MIC method. Section V showcases the simulation outcomes. Finally, Section VI concludes this article.
