A. Scenario 1: Probability Hypothesis Density
In the first simulation scenario, we compared the performance of our proposed MIC method with the AA and IF methods within a distributed framework that integrates the PHD filter and BE. The results of 50 Monte Carlo simulations are shown in Figs. 6 and 7. Fig. 6 depicts the estimated target cardinality over time, while Fig. 7 presents the OSPA performance. The results indicate the following.
1) As shown in Fig. 6, at least ten steps are needed for convergence in target estimation. The proposed MIC significantly accelerates the system’s ability to capture true target cardinality, particularly when five targets are simultaneously born, with PHD-MIC-IF achieving convergence within ten steps. In contrast, the AA shows a less pronounced advantage of MIC, accompanied by higher computational costs. This is attributed to clutter in the early stages overwhelm true targets, causing the loss of valid information. 2) After converging to the vicinity of the true target cardinality, Fig. 7 shows that under the PHD framework, target position estimation accuracy is comparable across methods. However, the two filters employing the MIC method exhibit smaller cardinality errors. Given the combined impact of substantial clutter and the irregular movement of targets, the system is more prone to misidentifying targets. The additional information derived from overlapping FoV via the MIC method helps mitigate this issue to some extent. 3) At Time = 22, 52, and 63, the target’s motion pattern underwent significant changes, with only the PHD-MIC-IF method achieving a smooth transition at Time = 22. In this process, the MIC played a role
