VI. CONCLUSION
For MTT scenarios, this article analyzes the information changes during the fusion process from the perspective of IC. This approach more intuitively demonstrates the impact of FoV overlaps on AA information fusion. In addition, based on the fact that sharing observation data from sensors with overlapping coverage areas can improve overall sensing performance, the MIC method was designed. It includes a high-speed clustering method indexed by local sensors, applied to JMPHD, effectively filtering out clutter and significantly enhancing the target identification speed of the sensor network. Simulation experiments show that the method achieves faster target recognition with a minimal increase in computation time, leading to quicker convergence. This has positive implications for fields such as cooperative search with adversarial characteristics. The BE method assumes accurate knowledge of the sensor’s FoV to construct the birth density at the boundaries. In scenarios where the FoV is not precisely known or is subject to uncertainty, the performance of the proposed method may degrade. Exploring adaptive or robust techniques to handle FoV uncertainties could address this limitation and represents an interesting direction for future work.
